<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/071Q">Example <span data-tag="071Q">9.7.6.7</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{Set}$ denote the category of sets. Then $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Set})$ is a presentable $\infty $-category, which is equivalent to the full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}^{\leq 0} \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ spanned by the $0$-truncated Kan complexes (see Remark <a data-tag="01Z2" href="/tag/01Z2">3.1.6.6</a> and Example <a data-tag="04V1" href="/tag/04V1">4.7.4.17</a>). For every presentable $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, Proposition <a data-tag="071N" href="/tag/071N">9.7.6.5</a> (applied in the special case $n = 0$) supplies an equivalence of $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Set}) \widehat{\otimes } \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\simeq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\heartsuit }$, where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\heartsuit } \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is the full subcategory spanned by the discrete objects. </p>
